# THE IDEAS LEDGERS ARE APPEND-MOSTLY, SO UNION IS THE RIGHT MERGE.
#
# Three workflows write .github/ideas/*.jsonl on their own schedules — pull adds
# papers to pool, review moves some into queue and marks the rest reviewed, post
# stamps one queue entry as posted. Each pushes to the same branch, and each
# rebases onto the remote when its push is rejected.
#
# Textual rebase cannot merge them. On 2026-07-29 a review run appended to
# queue.jsonl while a post run stamped the last line of it, git reported CONFLICT
# on the file, and the run died holding a half-finished rebase (run 30500800107).
# At one post an hour instead of one a day, that collision stops being unlucky.
#
# `merge=union` keeps BOTH sides' lines, which is exactly right for a ledger of
# one-JSON-object-per-line and cannot produce a conflict marker. What it CAN
# produce is the same record twice — once stamped, once not — when two runs edit
# one line. `scripts/ideas-dedupe.mjs` collapses that, and `scripts/ideas-push.sh`
# runs it after every rebase, so the pair is what makes this safe. Do not add one
# without the other.
.github/ideas/*.jsonl merge=union

# NOT seen.json. It is a single JSON object, and unioning two versions of it
# duplicates the closing brace and produces a file that will not parse. Only the
# daily pull writes it, so plain textual merge is both correct and sufficient.
